Terraform Commands Every Cloud & DevOps Admin Should Know 
If you're managing infrastructure as code, Terraform is likely a core part of your toolkit. 

Here are the essential Terraform commands every Cloud & DevOps admin must know. 
 
Note: These are not the only commands there are still a lot of commands, however these commands are used very frequently in terraform operations. 
 
 
1.    terraform –help= Displays help information and lists available commands and options for Terraform.
2.    terraform fmt=Prepare Terraform configuration files using a standard format or make the alignment of codes proper.
3.    terraform fmt –diff= Format Terraform configuration files and displays the updated version of the file without actually making the changes.
 
4.    terraform fmt –check= Verifies whether Terraform configuration files follow formatting guidelines without modifying them.
 
5.    terraform init= To initialize the terraform directory
 
6.    terraform init -lock=false = Initiates a new Terraform configuration or launches an existing one without gaining a lock on state files.
 
7.    terraform get -update= Updates modules to their latest versions, ensuring the configuration uses the most recent module releases.
 
8.    terraform validate= Checks the syntax and structure of Terraform configuration files and the code also.
 
9.    terraform plan= Generates an execution plan, detailing the actions Terraform will take to achieve the desired infrastructure state.
 
10. Terraform apply= to execute the plan state into execution/ deployment.
 
11. terraform apply --auto-approve= Automatically applies the execution plan without requiring manual confirmation.
 
12. terraform plan -destroy= Generates an execution plan specifically for destroying existing infrastructure resources defined in the Terraform configuration.
 
13. Terraform destroy= to destroy/ delete the resources declared in terraform configuration file. 
 
14. terraform destroy --auto-approve= Automatically destroys all Terraform-managed infrastructure without requiring manual confirmation
 
15. terraform refresh= to updates the Terraform state file.


16. Terraform init - - upgrade= to upgrade the terraform providers or after manual version change of provider.
 
17. terraform show= Displays the current state of Terraform-managed infrastructure.
 
18. terraform state=Manages the state of Terraform-managed infrastructure.
 
19. terraform state show <resource name/ block name>= Shows the full details of a specific resource in the state.
 
20. terraform state list= Displays all resources managed by Terraform within the current state file.
 
21. terraform providers= Displays a list of all available providers along with their versions
 
22. terraform workspace list= Displays a list of available workspaces
 
23. terraform workspace show= Displays the current workspace name being used in Terraform.
 
24. terraform force-unlock <lock_id>= Forcefully release a Terraform state lock.
25. terraform graph=Generates a visual representation of the dependency graph for Terraform resources
 
26. terraform console= Opens an interactive shell for evaluating expressions and test.
 
27. terraform -install-autocomplete= Enables shell autocompletion for Terraform commands
 
